course: dictionary
lesson: 3872
title: Frankly and honest words
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Frankly means honestly and openly. When someone says frankly, they are about to tell you what they really think, without hiding it. «Frankly, I don't think that drawing is finished.» The speaker is being honest about the drawing.
explain: Frankly often starts a sentence, like a signal. It tells you the next words are the speaker's true opinion. Sometimes that opinion is a bit blunt or surprising, so it can sound rude. Ask yourself: is this person being open, or are they hiding something?
explain: Fronted adverbials are words or phrases at the start of a sentence, followed by a comma. Some of them show the speaker's opinion, like Frankly, Luckily and Sadly. «Luckily, the bus was on time» tells you how the writer feels about the bus, just as «Frankly, the bus was late» tells you what the speaker honestly thinks.
fact: Frankly means honestly and openly, saying what you really think.
fact: Frankly is an adverb, so it tells us how someone speaks or acts.
fact: «Frankly, I don't care» is a blunt way of saying you are not interested.
fact: Phrases with frankly include «frankly speaking», «to put it frankly» and «quite frankly», which all mean honestly.
fact: Frankly can sound blunt, so it is often used when the speaker is about to say something that may upset someone.
exercise: «Frankly, I don't think your drawing is finished.» What does frankly show? | *The speaker is going to say something honest | The speaker is very tired | Hidden and secret | The speaker is singing a song
exercise: Ella says, «Quite frankly, the film was too long.» Which word means about the same as frankly? | Slowly | Happily | Quietly | *Honestly
exercise: In a story, a character who speaks frankly is | Very tired after a long walk | Hiding a secret under the bed | Silent and shy | *Open and honest about what they think
test: What kind of word is frankly in «Frankly, I think you should apologise.»? | *An adverb | A verb | A noun | An adjective
test: Mum says, «To put it frankly, dinner is burnt.» What does she mean? | Dinner is very hot | Dinner is quiet | *She is honestly saying dinner is ruined | Dinner is late
exam: Which sentence uses frankly to mean honest and open? | «The bird flew frankly over the hill.» | *«Frankly, I think you should say sorry.» | «The frankly cake was in the oven.» | «The sky was frankly blue and quiet.»
